      "writtenOn": "2022-11-22T02:37:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Wouldn\u0027t it be more flexible when src and snk extensions were arguments for vpd init? Let\u0027s say that there are two different source extensions. First supports only basic functionalities and the second supports more features. There may be one case where you want to test VPD with basic source functionalities and another with extended source features. Using source extension argument you can have both version of VPD without changing this code.\n\nIf this approach is too complicated, then I am fine with current version too.",
      "revId": "3730e41326ced83a96c3956fc5e06e25ca420192",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d6a563f5_db39a86c",
        "filename": "zephyr/emul/tcpc/emul_tcpci_partner_vpd.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 118,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-11-22T19:21:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It would be more flexible, but passing in all three sub-extensions was getting pretty complicated to get right, particularly since the order for some of them matters.\n\nI think even if I was passing in the source extension, the sink extension should still be captive/invisible. A VPD (at least as I\u0027m modeling it here) doesn\u0027t actually sink power over VBUS or respond to the messages that a Sink ordinarily would, but when you connect the VPD\u0027s host port first, the host port comes up as  Sink for the purpose of establishing PD communication. After the reconnection, it won\u0027t act as a sink anymore. There\u0027s probably a more accurate way to model it, but this seems good enough for now.\n\nThe source extension could be passed in. For the purpose of the test that I wanted to write, it doesn\u0027t matter what kind of source it is. Offhand, I can\u0027t think of a good test where it would matter, but we might need to revisit this when we start implementing PD compliance tests. I think we can make it easier to compose different partner types when we can use C++ properly (soon).\n\nFor now, I\u0027d like to keep this as is and hope we don\u0027t need to make it more complicated.",
      "parentUuid": "bd2a514b_3b622e44",
      "revId": "3730e41326ced83a96c3956fc5e06e25ca420192",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    }
  ]
